I'm joined today by Barry Friedman who  teaches at new york university school of  law where he's the director of the  policing project he also is the author  of unwarranted policing without  permission which will be out in February  a berry is so great to talk to you give  us first just a general sense of the  book unwarranted and the issues that you  deal with their sure David great to be  here today so unwarranted is the review  of policing from everything from the  beat cop so issues so to stop and frisk  racial profiling all the way up to the  NSA bulk data collection a lot of  electronic surveillance drone stingrays  covers all of that with a basic theory  about how people ought to be engaged in  what the police are doing is there some  broader thesis that ties together all of  these maybe seemingly unrelated to some  people aspects of policing and law  enforcement is there what are the trends  that you're pointing to sure there's one  key thesis that I think will surprise  most people which is that we've lived in  an environment over the last several  years where we're pointing fingers at  the police and blaming them for lots of  things that they're doing but in my book  I point the singer somewhere differently  in part of the course but primarily a  top that the American people my idea is  that we wanted to be safe but we don't  want to know how it happens when we do  it indicates that we're just content  with how it happens but it's our  obligation as is true elsewhere  democracy to be involved in the front  end from the start and does this relate  to Rio we've talked in in the wake of  revelations from Edward Snowden for  example there have been a number of  interesting pieces written which point  out that depending on how recently  there's been a sort of notable prominent  terrorist attack or news event that  triggers feelings of lack of safety and  wanting safety that that seems to have a  huge influence on the public at large in  terms of the degree of surveillance or  level of militarization of law  enforcement may be that the average  person is willing to accept is there  something to that sure I mean you put  your finger on a broader program is a  problem is true all the time around  legislation  salience you know we tend to pay  attention to what just happened  immediately and it's really big and  really important the problem is that in  the rest of government even though we  focus on salient events we kind of keep  a steady stream going of sensibility by  being involved in regulation all the  time when it comes to policing the  people are largely excluded from what  policing agencies do until there's an  explosion like that and what we need to  find a way to do is to be involved you  know on an ongoing basis and setting  helping sets of strategies figuring out  what works figuring out one of the  methods by which we want to be believed  one of the topics in the book I guess  generally i'll call it policing in  secret can you give us some examples of  what might fall under that umbrella sure  you know I use one example to start that  chapter and as you know all the chapters  start with stories about leasing has  kind of gone off the rails too often  expect affecting very ordinary folks in  terrible ways and I start that chapter  talking about stingrays so stingrays are  a form of device that the police used to  track cell phones and they can be  incredibly valuable incredibly important  for finding somebody who's been  kidnapped or somebody's on the run but  they also can be used incredibly  intrusive Lee to track demonstrators or  protesters and the thing about stingrays  is we the people had no knowledge that  they were being used the federal Justice  Department had worked with the  manufacturer to provide them to local  police helped fund them under agreements  that in tempted to say that their very  existence be kept secret even from  judges in cases when when when it gets  the police applied for a warrant or  something and so it started to leak out  and what happens when we keep things  secret is that when they leak out people  get annoyed they get angry and often  they get angry enough that we stop using  that kind of surveillance all together  so the thesis of the book is we need a  middle ground we need to be informed we  need to make sensible solutions on the  front end about how surveillance is  going to take place so they're all safe  but in a way in which we're comfortable  yeah as far as the Stingray I'm sure  you've read some of the the descriptions  of cases in which in order to shield the  existence and use of stingray technology  law enforcement official  were sort of simultaneously building a  sort of side by side or a concurrent of  case essentially so that by the time  they got to trial or at the time at  which they had to talk to a judge about  a warrant they had a sort of shadow  chain of evidence that hid the existence  of stingray technology do I have that  more or less right yeah it's incredible  they call it parallel construction yeah  so it's exactly what you just described  and the idea is we don't want anybody to  know we're doing this so once we learn  some information then we can construct  an alternate story about how we got  there you know it's always easier to  solve a maze by going in from the  solutions to the middle and so that's  pretty much what they do and what's  incredible and you have to understand  how bipartisan the concern about these  issues ought to be when it started to  leak out what really motivated change  was a joint letter from at the time that  sort of two heads of the Senate  Judiciary Committee one from the  Democratic side Patrick Leahy and the  other chuck Grassley basically writing  the FBI on tank what's going on and when  they got initial answers their responses  you know that's not enough we want to  know what's going on and that's that's  the right response we the people ought  to know upfront what's going on we ought  to weigh in we're not crazy we're very  sensible and then we ought to make  decisions about what's the right way to  be pleased and in terms of making those  decisions and as you talked about  finding a middle ground and being  upfront about the methods that are being  used I've interviewed many people who  are on different places I guess on the  ideological spectrum with regard to  policing and privacy and surveillance  etc what's your opinion as to whether  the demonstrable effectiveness of a  technique or piece of technology at  preventing crime should be considered  when evaluating whether it should be  allowed well let's use the most obvious  thing in the world to me what you just  said which is should we know how  effective something is before we permit  it or use it it's not just a question of  intrusion by the way we spend billions  of dollars on public safety and the one  thing we all want to be is safe so I'll  tell you something interesting I  I've started a not-for-profit to work on  these issues called the policing project  and one of our focuses on cost-benefit  analysis of policing so everywhere else  the government where there's a lot of  money being spent we try to answer the  simple question are we doing more good  than harm are we doing it a  cost-effective way are we being  effective in a way that does the least  damage if those are the most obvious  questions around policing and yet we  don't ask them there's almost no cost  benefit analysis Don look around  policing that we can learn a tremendous  amount now their reasons for this but  but as a bottom line conclusion the  answers gotta beat it does need to  change in the third part of the upcoming  book unwarranted you get into 21st  century policing and I think that this  relates to the cost-benefit analysis  significantly insofar as as technology  continues to improve and become more  ubiquitous new technologies the cost for  using those technologies continues to go  down do you think that that will play a  role in the types of surveillance  technology cloud storage government  databases etc that will be employed by  law enforcement in the coming years  today there's a great question and you  put it in an interesting way you are  absolutely right that the cost of  surveillance techniques will go down and  for that reason I think what we'll see  in the future is for example much more  in the way of surveillance and less  perhaps in the way of use of force which  occupies the minds of a lot of people  today because we'll just keep an eye on  folks that's good right right I'm sorry  that sounds good maybe at the surface  well you know there's huge promise in  these technologies which is to the  extent that they can help us focus on  and target bad guys we should all be in  favor of that but the one thing I did  want to say though is that when you talk  about the cost of surveillance you know  it's not just the cost of the gadget  that's what we forget around bleaching  the coffee it's the cost of the gadget  but then also any good cost-benefit  analysis without you know what about the  cost of privacy what about the cost of  security both security from crime but  also security for the government so  that's the kind of thing we have to get  better and analyzing as we move forward  into this brave new world but I would  agree with you that there's a terrific  opportunity here  how do you weigh or evaluate the value  of some of those intangibles especially  when the value of privacy may be  different to different people how can  you even start to dig into that issue so  again a great question and it's not easy  I think it's a primary reason we don't  do cost-benefit analysis now around  three thing is that we you know how to  tackle those hard questions but what I  will point out is that everything you  just you know suggestion with regard to  policing would have been equally true in  the environmental area 10 15 20 years  ago really hard questions about how to  weigh the use of progress and certain  substances or chemicals and society  against the risks of harm's sometimes  risks of harm that we don't know much  about that affect people in very  different ways but there's really smart  people out there in the world who work  on exactly these kinds of questions in  fact we're gathering a bunch of them  here at NYU Law School in February's to  start talking about it and the trick is  to start to try to you know tease them  apart and reach answers so that we're  spending our public safety dollars  lively last thing I'd like to touch on  is the Constitution when we talk about  21st century policing to use the term  from your book and Technology do the  constitutional issues get more complex  what is a search without a warrant when  you're using technology what is even a  search what constitutes a general search  versus a specific one it is this going  to get even muddier sure so the book as  you know is divided into three parts the  first about democratic policing the  second about constitutional policing in  the third about 21st century policing  and what I try to do in that last part  is show how if you get fixed in the  correct way on what the constitutional  issues are something that we often don't  do in the courts do a terrible job of if  you if you slow down and think about  what the constitutional rules are  generally then it gets easier to apply  them to the technologies that they come  but we really need to do that and courts  have made an absolute mess of this and  with the you know wave of technology  that's coming we've got to get our head  screwed on right about it I'll remind  you we've been talking about the book  unwarranted policing without permission  it's going to be out in  februari and we've been speaking with  the book's author Barry Friedman who  teaches at NYU School of Law thank you  so much for talking to us about this  been great agree with you David take  care

